  "root_cause": "The disclosure menu does not close when keyboard focus moves outside the menu via Tab, leaving the dropdown open and covering page content.",
  "repair_strategy": "Add a focusout listener on the root menu element that closes the associated controller (toggle) when focus leaves the menu and the controller is open, while ensuring focus movements inside the menu (e.g., from button to menu item) do not trigger a close.",
  "edits": [
    {
      "path": "src/disclosureMenu.js",
      "sha256": "ca4cd96d5ab9b2275e2c8f4989766af90595913fd63bb25320c2b0b8853b564b",
      "before": "    try {
      super.initialize();

      this._handleFocus();
      this._handleClick();
      this._handleHover();
      this._handleKeydown();
      this._handleKeyup();
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(error);
    }",
      "after": "    try {
      super.initialize();

      // Close dropdown when focus leaves the menu
      this.dom.menu.addEventListener('focusout', (event) => {
        const relatedTarget = event.relatedTarget;
        if (relatedTarget && !this.dom.menu.contains(relatedTarget)) {
          if (this.elements.controller && this.elements.controller.isOpen) {
            this.elements.controller.close();
          }
        }
      });

      this._handleFocus();
      this._handleClick();
      this._handleHover();
      this._handleKeydown();
      this._handleKeyup();
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(error);
    }"
    }
  ],
  "expected_accessibility_effect": "When a user tabs out of the navigation, the Shop dropdown will close, preventing it from obscuring underlying content and restoring the expected state (aria-expanded="false" and #plants hidden).",
  "functional_risks": [
    "Incorrectly closing the dropdown when focus moves from the button to a menu item (mitigated by checking that the relatedTarget is not inside the menu).",
    "Attempting to close when the controller is null or not open (guarded by conditional checks)."
  ],
  "verification_requirements": [
    "After fixing, tabbing from the Shop button to the search input should result in aria-expanded="false" on #shop and #plants hidden.",
    "Opening the dropdown via Enter should still work and allow navigation inside the dropdown with Tab.",
    "Shift+Tab from the first menu item back to the button should keep the dropdown open.",
    "Clicking outside the menu with the mouse should continue to close the dropdown (existing behavior unchanged)."
  ]
}

reasoning:
We need to analyze the problem.

The test scenario:

Frozen journey requirements:
Step 0: seek #shop (likely focusing the Shop button)
Step 1: press Enter -> expects aria-expanded="true" on #shop and visible #plants (dropdown opens)
Step 2: seek #search (tab out of navigation to search input) -> expects aria-expanded="false" on #shop and #plants invisible

Observed failure: At step 2, after tabbing to #search, the assertions fail because aria-expanded on #shop is still "true" (observed true expected false) and #plants visible (observed true expected false). So the dropdown remains open when focus leaves the navigation via tab.

Thus the issue: When focus moves out of the menu (via Tab), the dropdown should close automatically. In many accessible menus, pressing Tab when focus is on the last item (or moving focus outside the menu) should close the dropdown. Usually handled by a blur event on the menu or listening for focusout.

Looking at the code: We have a disclosure menu implemented via BaseMenu etc. There's likely missing handling for when focus leaves the menu (blur) to close open submenus.

Let's examine the relevant files: src/disclosureMenu.js (the main class), src/_baseMenu.js (base class), src/_baseMenuToggle.js, etc.

We need to find where focus changes are handled. In BaseMenu, there is _handleFocus method that sets focusState to self when a menu item gets focus. Also there is blur method etc.

But we need to close the dropdown when focus leaves the menu entirely (i.e., when focus goes to an element outside the menu). Possibly we need to add a focusout listener on the menu to close if focus leaves.

Look at _baseMenu.js: there is a method blur() that sets focusState to none and blurs the menu if shouldFocus. But that is called manually perhaps.

In _handleClick, there is a document pointerup listener that checks if click outside menu and closes children. That's for mouse clicks outside.

But for keyboard tab-out, we need to detect when focus leaves the menu (focusout) and close.

Let's search in the provided source for "focusout" or "blur" event listeners. I don't see any in the snippets.

In _baseMenu.js, there is _handleFocus method adding focus listener to each menu item. No blur listener.

There is also a method blur() that sets focusState to none and blurs the menu if shouldFocus.

Potential fix: Add a focusout listener on the menu element that, when focus leaves the menu (i.e., relatedTarget not inside menu), close the dropdown (if open). Or we could rely on the controller's blur? Actually the controller is the Shop button; when focus leaves the button to go to search, the button loses focus. Perhaps we need to close the dropdown when the controller loses focus and the menu is open.

In the disclosure pattern, when the trigger (button) loses focus and the popup is open, we should close it unless focus moved inside the popup. Since we are tabbing from button to search (outside), we should close.

Thus we can add a blur event listener to the controller (the Shop button) that checks if the menu is open and if the new focus is not inside the menu, then close.

Alternatively, add a focusout listener on the menu itself.

Let's look at where the controller is set: In BaseMenu.initialize(), if isTopLevel and controller and container exist, they create a BaseMenuToggle and set this._elements.controller = toggle.

Thus the controller is a BaseMenuToggle instance (DisclosureMenuToggle). It has open/close methods.

We could add an event listener to the controller's toggle element for blur/focusout.

Where to add? Perhaps in DisclosureMenu's initialize after calling super.initialize(), we can add a listener.

But we need to make minimal edit: modify src/disclosureMenu.js maybe.

Let's examine src/disclosureMenu.js source:

It defines class DisclosureMenu extends BaseMenu.

It has constructor, initialize, _handleClick, _handleKeydown, _handleKeyup, etc.

We can add a focusout listener to the controller's toggle element inside initialize after super.initialize().

But we need to ensure we only add for top-level menu (since this is the root menu). The controller exists only for top-level.

Thus in DisclosureMenu.initialize(), after super.initialize(), we can add:

if (this.elements.controller) {
  this.elements.controller.dom.toggle.addEventListener('focusout', (event) => {
    // If focus is leaving the controller and not going into the menu, close.
    const relatedTarget = event.relatedTarget;
    if (relatedTarget && !this.dom.menu.contains(relatedTarget)) {
      this.elements.controller.close();
    }
  });
}

But we need to consider that when focus moves from controller to a menu item (inside dropdown), relatedTarget will be the menu item, which is contained in this.dom.menu, so we should not close. When focus moves from controller to outside (like search), relatedTarget is not contained, so we close.

Also need to handle case when focus moves from menu item back to controller (shift+tab). That's fine.

Edge: When focus moves from menu item to outside (e.g., tab from last menu item to search), the controller may not lose focus directly; the focusout on controller won't fire because focus never left controller; it left the menu item. However, the menu item's blur might fire? We could also listen on the menu for focusout.

Simpler: Add a focusout listener on the menu element itself. When focus leaves the menu (i.e., relatedTarget not inside menu), close the dropdown via controller.

Because when focus moves from menu item to outside, the menu loses focus; we can close.

Thus in DisclosureMenu.initialize(), after super.initialize(), add:

this.dom.menu.addEventListener('focusout', (event) => {
  const relatedTarget = event.relatedTarget;
  if (relatedTarget && !this.dom.menu.contains(relatedTarget)) {
    // Focus leaving the menu
    if (this.elements.controller && this.elements.controller.isOpen) {
      this.elements.controller.close();
    }
  }
});

But we need to ensure we don't close when focus moves within the menu (e.g., from button to menu item). The relatedTarget will be the menu item, which is contained, so condition fails.

When focus moves from menu item to outside, relatedTarget is outside, condition passes, we close.

When focus moves from outside to menu (e.g., shift+tab into menu), relatedTarget is outside, but focus is entering menu; we shouldn't close. However, the focusout event fires on the element losing focus, which would be the previous element (outside) not the menu. So the menu's focusout won't fire on focus entering. Good.

Thus this should work.

